Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• cities.insert_date – filter rows that were inserted in January 2022 and order them by day to study “consecutive‑day” stretches.  
• cities.country_code_2 – group January 2022 rows by country and count how many different days each country appears on.  
• cities.capital – identify which January 2022 rows refer to the capital city (value = 1).  
• cities.city_name – (optional) lets us read the name of the capital city.  
• cities_countries.country_code_2 / country_name – translate the two‑letter code into the country’s full name once the target country is known.  

Conditions that will be useful  
• WHERE c.insert_date LIKE '2022-01-%'      – restrict to January 2022.  
• GROUP BY c.country_code_2  
• HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT DATE(c.insert_date)) = 9  
• To build “consecutive” runs: use DATE(c.insert_date) and window functions such as ROW_NUMBER() and JULIANDAY().  

Ten exploratory‑to‑final SQL queries (all capped with LIMIT 20 for safety)  
```sql
-- 1. Peek at January 2022 rows in the “cities” table
SELECT city_id, city_name, country_code_2, capital, insert_date
FROM cities
WHERE insert_date LIKE '2022-01-%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. List every country code that has any January 2022 inserts
SELECT DISTINCT country_code_2
FROM cities
WHERE insert_date LIKE '2022-01-%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Count how many DIFFERENT January 2022 days each country has data for
SELECT country_code_2,
       COUNT(DISTINCT DATE(insert_date)) AS jan_days
FROM cities
WHERE insert_date LIKE '2022-01-%'
GROUP BY country_code_2
ORDER BY jan_days DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Identify the country (code & name) that appears on NINE different January 2022 days
SELECT cc.country_code_2,
       cc.country_name,
       COUNT(DISTINCT DATE(c.insert_date)) AS jan_days
FROM cities AS c
JOIN cities_countries AS cc
      ON cc.country_code_2 = c.country_code_2
WHERE c.insert_date LIKE '2022-01-%'
GROUP BY cc.country_code_2, cc.country_name
HAVING jan_days = 9
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Show all January 2022 rows for that nine‑day country (replace '??' with the code you discover)
SELECT city_id, city_name, capital, insert_date
FROM cities
WHERE insert_date LIKE '2022-01-%'
  AND country_code_2 = '??'
ORDER BY insert_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. List ONLY the distinct January 2022 dates for that country, ordered
SELECT DISTINCT DATE(insert_date) AS d
FROM cities
WHERE insert_date LIKE '2022-01-%'
  AND country_code_2 = '??'
ORDER BY d
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Add row numbers so we can derive consecutive‑day “blocks”
WITH jan AS (
    SELECT DATE(insert_date) AS d,
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY DATE(insert_date)) AS rn
    FROM cities
    WHERE insert_date LIKE '2022-01-%'
      AND country_code_2 = '??'
    GROUP BY DATE(insert_date)
)
SELECT d,
       rn,
       JULIANDAY(d) - rn AS grp_key
FROM jan
ORDER BY d
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Collapse by grp_key to find length of every consecutive block
WITH jan AS (
    SELECT DATE(insert_date) AS d,
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY DATE(insert_date)) AS rn
    FROM cities
    WHERE insert_date LIKE '2022-01-%'
      AND country_code_2 = '??'
    GROUP BY DATE(insert_date)
),
blocks AS (
    SELECT MIN(d)  AS block_start,
           MAX(d)  AS block_end,
           COUNT(*) AS block_len
    FROM (
        SELECT d,
               (JULIANDAY(d) - rn) AS grp_key
        FROM jan
    )
    GROUP BY grp_key
)
SELECT *
FROM blocks
ORDER BY block_len DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Retrieve every city row that falls inside THE LONGEST consecutive block (replace :start and :end)
SELECT city_id, city_name, capital, insert_date
FROM cities
WHERE country_code_2 = '??'
  AND DATE(insert_date) BETWEEN :start AND :end
ORDER BY insert_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Compute the proportion of rows from the capital city within that longest block
WITH period AS (
    SELECT *
    FROM cities
    WHERE country_code_2 = '??'
      AND DATE(insert_date) BETWEEN :start AND :end
)
SELECT
    SUM(CASE WHEN capital = 1 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) * 1.0
      / COUNT(*)                       AS capital_share,
    COUNT(*)                          AS total_rows,
    SUM(CASE WHEN capital = 1 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS capital_rows
FROM period
LIMIT 20;
```

Replace  
• ‘??’ with the two‑letter code returned in query 4.  
• :start / :end with the block_start and block_end dates found in query 8.
